// Period Drama: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== period-drama.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Thread
genre: period-drama + coming-of-age
tone: Steam, discipline and a new kind of pride
era: 1873 | period
setting: A government silk-reeling mill, Tomioka, Japan | real | workplace
logline: The daughter of a samurai house that has lost its stipend is sent to work at the nation's first modern silk mill, to prove to the countryside that a factory will not kill a girl or shame her.
premise: The new government has built a brick mill with French machines and cannot fill it: the rumour is that the foreigners drink the girls' blood. So the daughters of former samurai are sent first, as examples. {ei}, sixteen, has been raised to pour tea and hold a halberd. She arrives with her family crest on her sleeve and is given a number.
synopsis: {ei} goes because her father asks, and because a household of proud people is selling its swords one at a time. The first act is the shock of the mill: bells instead of temple hours, three hundred basins of boiling cocoons, a French engineer {brunat} who drinks red wine in full view and so confirms the rumour, and an instructress, {marie}, who teaches by taking {ei}'s scalded hands and moving them. Rank is by skill, measured daily and posted. A weaver's daughter from the hills, {kiku}, is first-class within a month; {ei}, who has never been beaten at anything by a commoner, is third. The second act is her learning to want it. She finds the thread by feel at last; the pride she was raised in turns out to transfer. She and {kiku} become rivals and then the mill's best pair. A fever goes through the dormitory and the old rumour revives; families come to the gate to take daughters home. {ei}'s father writes: he has a marriage offer for her, to a man with money, and the experiment has been made. The mill's director asks the first-class girls to do what it has all been for, to go home and teach. In the third act {ei} makes the choice her upbringing did not offer: she declines the marriage in a letter of perfect formality, and takes the posting, to a new mill in her own impoverished province, as an instructress. {kiku} is sent to another. They part at the gate as equals, with a bow of exactly the same depth. The last scene is {ei} taking a farm girl's scalded hands and moving them.
question: What becomes of pride when the thing you were proud of is abolished?
themes: status remade as skill; women as the first industrial workforce; duty reinterpreted
audience: Adults and older teenagers; lovers of period detail
viewpoint: With {ei}; the mill is strange, then home
stakes: A family's standing, a government's experiment, and a girl's say in her own life
conflict: A samurai daughter's inherited rank against a place that ranks only by what her hands can do
ending: She refuses the marriage and goes home as a teacher of the trade. The friendship ends in a bow between equals.
experience: The satisfaction of a hard skill slowly got, a rivalry that warms, and a quiet act of self-determination
look: Red brick and glass in a landscape of thatch; rows of girls in steam; one white thread drawn out of a brown cocoon
sound: Bells, reels, water at the boil. A French song hummed by the engineer, and then by the girls.
m: rise | self | past-vs-future | transformed
@ ei | Okabe Sen | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 16 | To be first-class | She has never been less than first at anything | Her hands, and a commoner who is better | Proud, disciplined, privately terrified of failing in public | From rank by birth to rank by skill to choosing her own duty | Formal speech that loosens over the film | Crested kimono sleeve tied back with a work cord
@ kiku | Kiku | co-lead | rival | human | she/her | 15 | Wages, and never to go back to the hills | Her family eats on what she sends | She has no name anyone need respect | Quick, blunt, generous once respected | From rival to partner to equal | Country dialect; laughs loudly | Patched indigo, hands that already know thread
@ marie | Claire Morin | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 27 | To leave behind girls who can teach | She is far from Lyon and will go home in a year | She has no Japanese and they no French | Brisk, exacting, kind through her hands | From foreign oddity to a teacher bowed to | French; counts in Japanese | Grey dress, sleeves pinned, hair net
@ brunat | Henri Laurent | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 33 | A mill that runs to the standard of Lyon | He staked his name on it | The countryside thinks he is a monster | Proud, practical, oblivious | From confirming the rumour to pouring his wine out in public | Loud French, bad Japanese | Frock coat, a wine glass at the wrong moment
@ father | Okabe Kazuma | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 48 | To see his daughter safely placed | He has nothing left to give her | He cannot imagine a life he was not raised to imagine | Upright, loving, defeated | From sending her to being refused by her, with honour | Classical, spare | Faded formal dress, an empty sword rack
~ ei - kiku | samurai daughter and weaver's daughter | contempt and resentment | a bow of equal depth
~ ei - marie | pupil and instructress | hands taken and moved | the same gesture passed on
~ ei - father | daughter and father | obedience | a refusal written in perfect form
